/*****************************************************************************
    printFamily -- THE STABLE HALF of the print-family POP.
    Grammar minion round 2, 2026-07-30.

    WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
    ---------------------
    Tony is about to change the print sink fork in C++: widen the sink
    selection, route `cout` past the diversion check, add an `opCerr`
    (kantCorpus CLAIM KANT-13 as COMPLETED, and CLAIM KANT-23). That change
    has no POP. This file is half of it.

    THIS HALF CONTAINS NO `cout` AND NO `cerr`, ON PURPOSE. It exercises only
    the two keywords that ALREADY EXIST -- `print` and `string` -- across the
    whole spacing and diversion matrix. Every row in it is GREEN TODAY and
    MUST STILL BE GREEN AFTER TONY'S CHANGE.

    That is the entire design idea, and it is the answer to "how does a
    fixture survive an implementation change": THE HALF THAT MOST NEEDS TO
    SURVIVE SURVIVES BY NOT PARTICIPATING. Nothing here grafts anything,
    nothing here names a keyword that does not exist yet, so nothing here has
    a before-state and an after-state. It is a pure regression net around the
    fork being widened -- if broadening the sink selection disturbs `print` or
    `string` in any way (spacing, shortcuts, diversion, the value path), the
    byte-exact diff catches it and names the row.

    The moving rows -- `cout`, `cerr`, and the omitted `string` form -- live
    in the SIBLING file `incant/printFamilyNew`, whose target is a
    `.divergence` because it is a PINNED WRONG ANSWER. Do not merge the two.

    ORACLE: existing behaviour, byte-exact. Same trick genLadder/spell.target
    plays on emitLeaf -- the current implementation is the fixture for the
    next one.

        genLadder/printFamily.target     stdout, byte-exact
        genLadder/printFamily.captured   stderr, byte-exact (the diverted
                                         buffer, flushed at the end)

    WHY TWO STREAMS AND NOT ONE
    ---------------------------
    A diverted `print` produces NOTHING on stdout, so the armed rows would be
    an absence-only assertion, and pop.sh's own rStuff comment is right that
    an absence check passes by being deleted. Flushing the buffer turns them
    into a PRESENCE assertion: the bytes have to show up, in order, in
    printFamily.captured.

    The flush goes to /dev/stderr and NOT /dev/stdout, and that is MEASURED,
    not stylistic. A buffer flushed to /dev/stdout is an unbuffered write
    while `print` goes through block-buffered tok `cout`, so the whole flush
    lands at the TOP of the capture, before every line that logically
    preceded it. Sharpens kantCorpus KANT-12's UPDATE, which recorded
    ordering-across-the-flush as unsettled: for /dev/stdout it is settled and
    it is wrong. See grammarCorpus CLAIM GRAM-9.

    THE SENTINEL IS LOAD-BEARING -- READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A ROW
    -------------------------------------------------------------
    An incant parse failure ABANDONS THE REST OF THE FILE AND STILL EXITS 0
    (CLAIM GRAM-8). A row that stops parsing does not fail loudly; it silently
    deletes every row after it while the harness reports exit 0. That is
    CLAUDE.md's "exited 0 is not passed" hazard in its purest form.
    So: the last statement before stop() is a SENTINEL, and it is the reason
    this file may be trusted at all. If the sentinel is missing from
    printFamily.target, the run TRUNCATED -- do not chase the row that
    diffed, find the row that stopped parsing.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
search reset Grokking list;
register(PrintFamily);

define
    divBuf buffer;
    vDef; vDollar; vUnder; vArmed;
    ;

print "PF ==== 1. print / UNARMED ====":;

/*  keyword print x diversion UNARMED x spacing {default, $, $ with _}

    ⚠ EVERY `$` HERE IS BALANCED -- one to turn default spacing OFF, one at
    the end of the same statement to turn it back ON -- and that is REQUIRED
    since 2026-07-31, not decoration. `useDefaultSpace = true` was removed
    from opPrint/opString, so `$` is a TOGGLE THAT PERSISTS. processAction
    resets it before each action runs, so it cannot leak into one, but these
    rows are TOP-LEVEL statements with no action between them: an unbalanced
    `$` on the "dol" row would leave spacing off, and the `$` on the "und"
    row would then turn it back ON and that row would silently stop testing
    what it is named for. The persistence itself is pinned separately, by
    incant/stringT row 4 -- keep the two fixtures doing different jobs.    */
print "PF-P-U-def" 1 2 3:;
print $"PF-P-U-dol" "joined"$:;
print $"PF-P-U-und" _ "spaced"$:;

print "PF ==== 2. string / UNARMED ====":;

/*  keyword `#` (the string expression) x diversion UNARMED x spacing
    {default, $, $ with _}
    A `#` expression builds a VALUE, so the value is printed back to make it
    observable. The print that reveals it is deliberately plain.

    ⚠ `#` REPLACED THE `string` KEYWORD on 2026-07-31 (Tony, offline).
    The grammar is `StringXP "#"- stuff=PrintXP+ defer;`. It was tried as
    `,` first and that COLLIDED with the shortcut set -- see incant/hashProbe
    for the crash and the controlled comparison that settled it. The point of the change is that a string expression and a print
    now share ONE vocabulary, shortcuts included, instead of ExpressioN
    needing a shortcut vocabulary of its own.                              */
vDef = #"PF-S-U-def" 1 2 3;
print "[" vDef "]":;
vDollar = #$"PF-S-U-dol" "joined"$;
print "[" vDollar "]":;
vUnder = #$"PF-S-U-und" _ "spaced"$;
print "[" vUnder "]":;

print "PF ==== 3. ARMING DIVERSION ====":;
printTO(divBuf);

/*  keyword print x diversion ARMED x spacing {default, $, $ with _}
    These three MUST NOT appear on stdout. They must appear, in this order,
    in printFamily.captured. That is KANT-23: diversion belongs to print.   */
print "PF-P-A-def" 1 2 3:;
print $"PF-P-A-dol" "joined"$:;
print $"PF-P-A-und" _ "spaced"$:;

/*  keyword `#` x diversion ARMED.
    A `#` expression has no stream, so a diversion must not touch it. The
    value is built here, INSIDE the armed window, and read back OUTSIDE it. */
vArmed = #$"PF-S-A-und" _ "spaced"$;

printTO(null);
print "PF ==== 4. DIVERSION RELEASED ====":;
print "[" vArmed "]":;

/*  the release must be complete -- this one is on stdout again  */
print "PF-P-R-def" 1 2 3:;

/*  Flush the captured buffer to fd 2. Everything above that was diverted
    lands in printFamily.captured, in order, HERE.                          */
divBuf modedOP "/dev/stderr";
closeFile(divBuf);

print "PF SENTINEL -- run reached the end; if this line is absent the run TRUNCATED":;
stop();
